I was looking for a new database solution for my iOS and macOS apps. The original idea was pretty simple: bring the power O love about postgres onto the client side.<p>I never liked CoreData. A long time ago I was using Realm because it solved my problem with slow batch insertions and brought reactivity. Nowadays there&#x27;s SwiftData too, but I&#x27;m honestly not a fan of it at all.<p>I&#x27;m in love with the server-side approach of working with SQL, like what I use with Vapor and what I originally built my SwifQL for.<p>Then I found DuckDB, which kind of shares my love for postgres-style SQL and gives you a looooooot more power to play with on the client side.<p>SuckDB already has an official Swift client, but it wasn&#x27;t the application-level API I wanted to use in my apps. So I decided to build my own Swift layer around it.<p>I&#x27;d already doing similar things with Bridges and SwifQL on the server side, so I brought a lot of those ideas with me.<p>And here&#x27;s what we have now: a beautiful, very Swiftly way of working with Duck and writing SQL:<p>```
import Duck<p>struct Todo: Table {
    @Column(&quot;id&quot;)
    var id: UUID<p><pre><code>    @Column(&quot;name&quot;)
    var name: String</code></pre>
}<p>let todos = try await Todo.all(on: .app)
```<p>There are more examples in the repo&#x27;s readme, together with SwiftUI and UIKit example apps you can immediately play with.<p>This is the first stable 1.0.0 release and it includes Codable integration, async&#x2F;await, reactive queries, migrations, transactions, reusable prepared statements, connection pooling, bulk ingestion through DuckDB&#x27;s Appender, native LIST&#x2F;STRUCT values, and the SwiftStream&#x2F;SQL DSL.<p>Underneath it is the real, fresh DuckDB 2.0.0 engine.<p>Right now Duck targets iOS15+ and macOS12+. I&#x27;ve also tested it on Linux. The engine itself supports many more platforms, but I haven&#x27;t validated the Duck on those yet.<p>I wanted a richer SQL engine with a beautiful Swift DSL, and I got it.<p>I you also want to try something different on the client side, welcome to the Duck club.<p>And since this is HN, here&#x27;s a problem I haven&#x27;t solved to my satisfaction yet.<p>If you want to hear about any problems... I haven&#x27;t found a silver bullet for the server-side migrations approach problem yet, so I kept migrations at the foundation level, and I&#x27;m going to experiment with one or more higher-level approaches on to of that. If you&#x27;ve dealt with the same problem, I&#x27;d genuinely love to hear how you approached it.

Hey HN,<p>I maintain a few Mac apps as side projects. Most of them involve permissions of some kind (microphone, screen recording) and I&#x27;ve always been annoyed by the abrupt permission granting popup that Mac will pop up whenever you request access:<p>&quot;MyApp would like to access your microphone&quot;
Don&#x27;t allow | Allow<p>&quot;Allow MyApp to access devices on the local network&quot;
Don&#x27;t allow | Allow<p>etc etc<p>I&#x27;m empathetic to Apple wanting to shock people out of their complacency - but the experience feels below the standard that people expect from their native apps. It feels almost adversarial.<p>The Codex Computer Use onboarding was far better. It pops up a hovering window below the System Settings screen that you drag into the list. At this point you&#x27;ll just be asked to confirm your password&#x2F;TouchID, since the OS knows you&#x27;re trying to add the specific app to a specific permission category. It does the same thing if you manually + add an app from your disk.<p>I hadn&#x27;t seen this paradigm anywhere else, but it seemed like the obvious way to improve the experience while still staying within the operating system constraints. I reproduced the underlying logic in Swift and bundled it into a library to check for your current permissions &amp; display a managed window to request whichever ones your app needs. You&#x27;re also welcome to import the authorization buttons explicitly so you can style them to your app&#x27;s need.<p>The Github GIF will do a better job of explaining the full UX. If you end up using it drop me a line.
